; bl/json_native.bl — the native half of bl.json: the byte loop, in Rust.
;
; ── Why this namespace exists at all ─────────────────────────────────────────
;
; `bl.json` DECIDES; this is the seam where the bytes get WRITTEN. That split is the
; entire design: the value mapping, key ordering, `:pretty`, `:ascii`, the record wire
; registry and every RFC 6901 path stay in `bl.json`, where policy belongs. Walking a
; term and writing its text happens here, in Rust, because a beam-lisp call costs
; ~0.7–1.0 us (measured) and a serialiser needs several per node — which is why the
; same job assembled in bl costs 16–23 ms per 24.3 KB where this costs 0.43–0.59 ms.
;
; MEASURED, same process, medians of 51, 24.3 KB, on a loaded host — read the RATIO as
; the finding and the millisecond as an anecdote. The same decode has read 0.83, 0.99, 1.12
; and 1.92 ms inside ONE session, so a single before/after pair proves nothing:
;
;                 bl.json today   this crate     Jason     OTP default
;   encode        14–23 ms        0.2–0.45 ms    0.24 ms   —
;   decode        2.1 ms          0.9–1.2 ms     0.2–0.3   0.14 ms
;
; The 3–10x over assembling in bl is real and is why this crate exists. Encode is at parity
; with Jason. DECODE IS NOT SETTLED: this crate is ~4x SLOWER than Jason's pure-ELIXIR
; parse of the same document, which is the wrong way round for a NIF. It has been
; decomposed, and the two obvious explanations are both DEAD (FEAT-043):
;
;   term construction — an ETF round trip of the SAME vectors and maps   0.05 ms  not the cost
;   removing `arbitrary_precision` — rebuilt, measured, compared         ~0       not the cost
;   OTP's own parser, same document                                      0.14 ms
;   Jason's own parser, same document                                    0.20–0.32 ms
;   this crate's parse                                                   0.9–1.2 ms
;
; So the time is in the PARSE — not in building Erlang terms, and not in the feature that
; was blamed first. Nothing here gets optimised again without a profile.
;
; ── It is REQUIRED, and that is a build invariant ────────────────────────────
;
; `bl.json` has no second compact encoder any more: the OTP assembler was DELETED, so there
; is nothing to fall back TO. `loaded?` reports whether the byte loop is present; the walk
; in `bl.json` runs only for `:pretty`, `:ascii`, and the shapes this crate refuses by
; design — a Set, a record, an integer past u64, nesting past 128. That walk is the
; AUTHORITY for those cases, not a hedge about packaging.
;
; Which makes a missing .so a LOUD problem, and it is not loud yet: `bl build` can fail
; reporting only "cargo produced no cdylib" (FUP-100), and the sole visible symptom is a
; boundary that is correct and ~40x slower. Verified by hand: `cargo build --release` in
; this directory, then copying the .so into priv/native/, restores it — that is how every
; number above was taken while `bl build` was lock-contended by another session.
;
; ── What the crate refuses, on purpose ───────────────────────────────────────
;
; A bl Set, a record, a foreign struct, or an integer beyond u64 (a NIF cannot
; stringify an Erlang bignum — rustler exposes no way to read one). Each RAISES rather
; than emitting `{"__struct__": …}`, so `bl.json` can handle it in beam-lisp with the
; helper's own error. Silently serialising a struct's internals is how a wrong document
; gets written, and a wrong document is worse than a slow one.

(ns bl.json-native
  (:require [vm.native :as vn]))

; The host module is `Elixir.BeamLisp.Native.Bl.JsonNative`, which is also the string
; the crate passes to `rustler::init!`. Those two must agree or `load_nif` binds nothing
; and EVERY call raises `:nif_not_loaded` while the .so sits there looking correct.
(defnative "bl_json"
  ; Arities are the BEAM arity — the `Env` a NIF takes is implicit — and they must match
  ; the Rust signatures EXACTLY. The decode NIF gained the key policy and became arity 2;
  ; leaving this at 1 made `load_nif` bind NOTHING, so every call kept raising
  ; `:nif_not_loaded` and `bl.json` quietly used its slow paths. A wrong arity is not a
  ; wrong-answer bug, it is a silently-absent-capability bug, and the test suite does not
  ; notice because the slow paths are correct — they are just slow.
  (json-encode 1)
  (json-decode 2)
  ; PARSE-ONLY probe, same build as the two above: it exists so the Rust/BEAM boundary cost
  ; can be MEASURED rather than argued (`bench/json.bl`). Removing it from this list does
  ; not break it in isolation — it breaks load_nif for EVERY function, because the
  ; declaration is all-or-nothing.
  (json-count 1))

(defn loaded?
  "Whether the native byte loop is present.

   The answer lives in `:persistent_term` rather than in a namespace-level `def`. The
   first version used a `def` holding an atom, and loom's suite failed 21 assertions with
   `undefined var: bl.json-native/present` — but the cause was NOT the `def`: a STALE AOT
   cache entry (in `~/.cache/beam_lisp/aot`) still referenced the old var, and clearing
   the cache made it green with the `def` still in place. Recorded because the wrong
   lesson is the tempting one. `persistent_term` is kept anyway on its own merits: this is
   a fact about the VM, a VM-wide store is where such facts belong (the record registry
   does the same), and it keeps mutable state out of a namespace that `bl.json` requires.

   A one-way LATCH, deliberately not a memoised `false`. The host module's `@on_load` runs
   exactly once per VM: if it ran while the .so was absent or stale, `load_nif` bound
   nothing and that host module never tries again. So a cached `false` could only ever be
   stale, while a cached `true` cannot be. The re-ask happens only when the native is
   ABSENT — on the slow path, where it is invisible."
  []
  (if (erlang/apply :persistent_term :get (list :bl-json-native-present false))
    true
    (let [now (vn/available? "bl.json-native")]
      (when now (erlang/apply :persistent_term :put (list :bl-json-native-present true)))
      now)))
